Icon sets for the 'marker-symbol' and 'stroke-symbol' attributes of a feature.

A value with a slash is the path of an icon, for example /icon-sets/fontawesome/mug-hot.png. A
value that starts with an ASCII letter is the name of a pinhead icon, for example
handled_teacup_and_saucer, or a maki name (see the links below). No emoji starts with an ASCII
letter. A whole number like 5 or 17 is a pinhead number. A keycap emoji like 5⃣ is a digit plus
a combining mark, so it stays an emoji. Any other value is an emoji character, and it is
rendered with the image of the same name in noto/. Two places apply this rule and must stay the
same: symbolUrl in app/javascript/helpers/functions.js and symbolUrlExpression in
app/javascript/maplibre/styles/styles.js.

Sets in the emoji picker
------------------------

pinhead     CC0, https://github.com/waysidemapping/pinhead , the icon set of the map and the
            first category of the picker. It redrew Maki, Temaki and eleven more sources to
            one style, so those sets are not included separately any more.
noto        Noto Color Emoji, https://fonts.google.com/noto/specimen/Noto+Color+Emoji
            The picker reads noto/emoji-mart-data.json for its emoji categories.
openmoji    CC BY-SA 4.0, https://openmoji.org/ , an alternative rendering of the emoji set,
            plus the extra icons that OpenMoji draws beyond Unicode, like emergency services
            and brand logos. The script reads their names from the data of OpenMoji 15.0.0,
            the version of the pngs.
mapforge    the icons that Mapforge makes itself: the numbers 13 to 50, composed from pinhead
            digits. The picker shows them in the pinhead category, through the links.
fontawesome CC BY 4.0, https://fontawesome.com/ , the free "solid" style only. The free
            package also has a "regular" (thin) style of the same icons and a "brands" style
            of colored logos, neither is in the index.

Every set except noto has an index.json, which the picker reads as one more category. The
files in public/ have a short cache time: 3 days in production and 1 day in development, see
config/environments. After an update of an index.json or an icon, a browser can show the old
file until then.

To add a set, add it to SETS in build_icon_sets.rb and to iconSets and categories in
app/javascript/controllers/feature/edit_controller.js. The script needs npm, inkscape and
imagemagick.

pinhead and fontawesome are monochrome SVG sets. The script downloads the npm package of a set
and rasterizes its icons to 72px PNG. It then keeps only the alpha channel, so that the icon is
white: a marker draws it on a circle in the color of the feature. A white icon needs a
background of its own on the page, see whiteIconSets in edit_controller.js.

Pinhead renamed the icons that it took over: maki 'cafe' is pinhead
'handled_teacup_and_saucer'. Its dist/changelog.json records the source name per icon, and the
script turns those names into search keywords, so a search for 'cafe' still finds the icon. The
same file gave the pinhead ids in app/javascript/maplibre/import/google_icons.js.

The script also writes relative symbolic links into pinhead/:

- One link per maki name, for example cafe.png -> handled_teacup_and_saucer.png, because
  Mapbox simplestyle GeoJSON uses maki names in marker-symbol. Only maki names get links:
  the names of the other sources would clash with each other. A maki name that is also a
  pinhead id keeps the pinhead icon, maki 'bus' shows pinhead 'bus'.
- One link per id that a pinhead release renamed, for example five.png -> 5.png. A saved
  feature keeps the path of the icon that it got from the picker, so an update must not
  break the old paths.
- One link per number that Mapforge composed, for example 17.png -> ../mapforge/17.png.

index.json lists the composed numbers and no other links, so the picker shows every icon once. A build deletes every png
first: a removed icon must not stay behind, and inkscape would write a new png through a
link of the same name onto another icon. The links are committed as git symlinks, a checkout
on Windows needs core.symlinks. spec/requests/icon_sets_spec.rb fails on a dangling link.

Pinhead draws the numbers 0 to 12, and Mapforge offers 0 to 50. The script composes 13 to 50
from two pinhead digits (see compose_numbers in build_icon_sets.rb). It takes the narrow 0, 1
and 2 of the pinhead 10 to 12, and narrows the other digits. An outline gives the narrowed
digits back half of the stroke width that they lost, so they look like the pinhead numbers
and keep their holes open. It writes them into mapforge/, the folder of the
icons that Mapforge makes itself, and links them from pinhead/. A marker-symbol '17' then
loads pinhead/17.png like any other number. The picker lists the numbers 0 to 50 first, in
their order.
The letters a to z need no links: pinhead has an icon of each name.

openmoji is an emoji set of PNG files. The script builds its index from the names and the
keywords of noto/emoji-mart-data.json, and it takes the png of the same emoji character.

Emoji sets
----------

The emoji sets are downloaded by hand. Noto releases are at
https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-emoji/releases (directory png/72). OpenMoji releases are at
https://github.com/hfg-gmuend/openmoji/releases (directory color/72x72). After a download, the
script convert_to_emoji_names.sh renames the files from their code points to the emoji
character itself. .gitignore drops the files that keep their original name.
